# PostgreSQL Container Shared Memory (`/dev/shm`)

## What the error means

- Message like **`could not resize shared memory segment "/PostgreSQL.<digits>"`** with **`No space left on device`** refers to the **container’s tmpfs `/dev/shm`**, not necessarily host disk space on `/var/lib/postgresql/data`.
- PostgreSQL uses shared memory segments backed by **`shm_size`** on the **db** service in Compose.

## What to do

- Increase **`shm_size`** on the Timescale/Postgres service (e.g. in `docker-compose.yaml` under `db:`) until heavy queries and parallel workers stop failing. Default compose may only document the knob in comments—set an explicit value (for example **1gb**–**4gb** depending on workload) when parallel workers or large sorts fail with `/dev/shm` errors.
- After changing **`shm_size`**, recreate the db container so the limit applies.

## Code interaction

- Django only surfaces the failure as **`django.db.utils.OperationalError`**; the root cause is **container runtime configuration**, not application Python logic.

## Overlap

- Composes with **`local-compose-db-lifecycle-for-web-tests.mdc`** / **`full-test-with-db-redis.mdc`** when validating DB health after compose edits.
